--TEST--
`Core\Http\Client::post` cannot ask for retries without `retryIdempotencyKey`, and it is the verb that decides — `rule:http-server/a-non-idempotent-retry-needs-an-idempotency-key`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:http-server/a-non-idempotent-retry-needs-an-idempotency-key`: `GET`, `HEAD`, `PUT` and `DELETE` retry freely, because a
// repeated one is a second question; `POST` and `PATCH` repeat an *effect*, so
// their retries need an `Idempotency-Key` the server can recognise, and this
// case asks the first of those two. Both halves of that
// question are written at the call — the verb is the member's own name, and ADR
// 0063 R2 makes the bag a literal — so it is refused while compiling, and no
// request is made to discover it.
string $url = "https://example.test/orders";

var $a = Core\Http\Client::post($url, {retryAttempts: 3});

// The depth claim is *agreement*: the same bag asked of every verb that repeats
// nothing, so a refusal written one condition too wide fails here rather than at
// a review.
var $b = Core\Http\Client::get($url, {retryAttempts: 3});
var $c = Core\Http\Client::put($url, {retryAttempts: 3});
var $d = Core\Http\Client::delete($url, {retryAttempts: 3});
var $e = Core\Http\Client::head($url, {retryAttempts: 3});

// And the key is what makes the retry legal, rather than `retryAttempts` being
// unwritable on a `post` at all.
var $f = Core\Http\Client::post($url, {retryAttempts: 3, retryIdempotencyKey: "order-7"});
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0796]: `Core\Http\Client::post` asks for retries without `retryIdempotencyKey`
   --> case.nvs:11:40
%A
   = help: a repeated `POST` is a second effect rather than a second question, so an attempt needs an identity the server can recognise: add `retryIdempotencyKey: "..."` to the same bag, sent as `Idempotency-Key` and identical across attempts
%A
error: aborting due to 1 error
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
